<div class="article-body">

<p>I haven't bought lettuce from a store in 9 months. Not because I'm a gardening genius. Because store lettuce is $4 a head now and the stuff I grow tastes like it was picked 30 seconds ago. Because it was.</p>

<p>Lettuce is the single easiest crop to grow indoors. It's fast (30 to 45 days). It's compact (fits on a shelf). It tolerates low light (less power than tomatoes). And it <strong>grows back after you cut it.</strong> One plant gives you salads for 8 to 12 weeks. Six plants give you a salad every single day.</p>

<h2 id="why-lettuce">Why Lettuce?</h2>

<p>Average household spends $120 to $180/year on salad greens. Most of that is water and shipping costs disguised as "organic premium." Growing your own cuts that to about $15/year in seeds and nutrients.</p>

<p>But the real reason isn't money. Store bought lettuce is nutritionally bankrupt by the time you eat it. A study in the Journal of Agricultural Food Chemistry showed lettuce loses 30 to 50% of its antioxidants within 24 hours of harvest. Store lettuce was picked 2 to 3 weeks ago.</p>

<h2 id="soil">Method 1: The "$8 Pot and Dirt" Special</h2>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> about $8. <strong>Time:</strong> 5 to 6 weeks. <strong>Difficulty:</strong> 1/10</p>

<p>You don't need special equipment. A pot, some soil, and a window. But window light alone won't grow full heads in winter. You'll get leggy, pale plants. Add a cheap <a href="/posts/best-led-grow-lights-for-beginners">LED grow light</a> and it's unstoppable.</p>

<p>What you need:</p>
<ul>
  <li>6 inch pot with drainage holes (any thrift store has these for $1)</li>
  <li>Standard potting mix. NOT garden soil. It compacts in pots and kills roots.</li>
  <li>Lettuce seeds. Black Seeded Simpson or Buttercrunch work best.</li>
  <li>Grow light or sunny south facing window.</li>
</ul>

<p><strong>The trick.</strong> Plant 4 to 5 seeds per pot. Once they sprout (3 to 5 days), thin to the 2 strongest seedlings. Don't throw the extras away. Transplant them to another pot. More plants = more salads.</p>

<h2 id="kratky">Method 2: The Mason Jar Hack ($12)</h2>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> about $12. <strong>Time:</strong> 4 to 5 weeks. <strong>Difficulty:</strong> 2/10</p>

<p>This is the cheapest hydroponic setup that actually works. A quart mason jar, a net cup, clay pebbles, and nutrient solution. No pumps. No moving parts. No electricity except the light.</p>

<p>Fill the jar 3/4 with nutrient water. Insert the net cup with a seedling. The roots grow down into the water. As the water level drops, air roots form. It self regulates. You refill every 2 to 3 weeks.</p>

<p>I grew a basil plant in a pickle jar using this method that hit 2 feet tall. My friends thought it was a decoration until I made pesto with it.</p>

<h2 id="dwc">Method 3: The "Serious Salad" Setup ($50)</h2>
<p><strong>Cost:</strong> about $50. <strong>Time:</strong> 4 to 5 weeks. <strong>Difficulty:</strong> 3/10</p>

<p>This is what I use for my main setup. A 5 gallon bucket, an aquarium air pump, an air stone, and 4 net cups on a lid. The pump bubbles oxygen into the nutrient water. Roots grow 3x faster than in soil. Harvestable heads in 30 days.</p>

<p>The pump cost $15 and runs 24/7. My electricity bill went up about $1.50/month. Worth it for $80/month in grocery savings.</p>

<h2 id="harvest">The Harvest Hack Nobody Tells You</h2>

<p>Don't pull the whole plant. Use the <strong>cut and come again method.</strong> Snip 2 to 3 outer leaves from each plant. Leave the inner rosette alone. New leaves grow back in 3 to 5 days. You can harvest like this for 8 to 12 weeks from one plant.</p>

<p>The moment a lettuce plant starts growing a tall center stalk, it's about to turn bitter. That's called bolting. Harvest the whole head immediately. You've got 2 to 3 days before it's inedible.</p>

<h2 id="troubleshoot">5 Problems (And 30 Second Fixes)</h2>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠️ "My seedlings are tall and falling over"</div>
  <p><strong>Not enough light.</strong> Move your light closer or add more hours. Seedlings need 14 to 16 hours. At 12 hours they stretch.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠️ "Leaves are yellow, not dark green"</div>
  <p><strong>Overwatered (soil) or pH off (hydro).</strong> Let soil dry between watering. In hydro, check pH. It should be 5.5 to 6.5.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠️ "Nothing is happening after 2 weeks"</div>
  <p><strong>Too cold.</strong> Lettuce stops growing below 55F. Ideal range is 60 to 70F. If your house is cold, add a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=seedling+heat+mat&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">seedling heat mat</a>.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠️ "Tastes bitter"</div>
  <p><strong>Too much light or bolting.</strong> Harvest earlier or move the light further away. Bitter lettuce = stressed lettuce.</p>
</div>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">⚠️ "White fuzzy stuff on soil"</div>
  <p><strong>Surface mold.</strong> Scrape it off. Let soil dry out more between watering. It's harmless to the plant but ugly.</p>
</div>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

<p>Pick one method and start today. The mason jar Kratky method costs $12 and will have you eating your own lettuce in 5 weeks. Five weeks from now you'll have spent another $20 on store salad anyway. Might as well spend $12 and never buy it again.</p>

